The text is given page by page; each page starts with its number, written [p. N]. The bibliography has been removed.

For each criterion, inclusion and exclusion alike, in the order above, give exactly one assessment, even when another criterion already decides, in this order:
1. "evidence_quote": the exact words of the text the assessment rests on, copied as they are written (original language, at most about 40 words, within one page if you can), and "page": the number N of the page they are on. Leave both empty when the text says nothing about the criterion. Never quote words that are not in the text, and never paraphrase inside a quote.
2. "finding": one short sentence saying what the report shows about the criterion. For an exclusion criterion, say whether the reason for exclusion is true of the report.
3. "status", which follows from the finding:
   - for an inclusion criterion: "met" when the report satisfies it; "not_met" when it clearly does not;
   - for an exclusion criterion: "met" when the reason for exclusion is true of the report, so that it should be excluded for this reason; "not_met" when the reason clearly does not apply. Read the criterion as written, negative wording included: an exclusion criterion such as "Did not study X" is "met" when the report does not study X, and "not_met" when it does;
   - "cannot_tell" when the text does not say.

Criteria on the publication itself (date, language, type of publication) are judged on the report as published: a report is published when it first appears, online or in print. The dates when its data were collected or when the manuscript was received, revised or accepted do not count. Compare dates exactly: a year within the limits of a date criterion, limits included, satisfies it.

Judge a criterion on what the report studies, not on how its authors frame it: a report can satisfy a criterion without using the words of the criterion. When the report addresses the topic of a criterion only in part, weigh whether a careful reviewer would consider it satisfied; if you cannot decide, the criterion is "cannot_tell".

Then give, in agreement with your assessments:
- "rationale": two or three sentences naming the decisive criteria by their codes, written in the language identified by this ISO 639-1 code: {{ language }}. A report is excluded only for an inclusion criterion that is "not_met" or an exclusion criterion that is "met".
- "decision": "include", "exclude" or "uncertain". Exclude only when at least one inclusion criterion is "not_met" or one exclusion criterion is "met". If no criterion is failed in this way and some cannot be told, the decision is "uncertain".
- "inclusion_probability": the probability, from 0 to 1, that a careful reviewer applying these criteria would include this report. Below 0.5 only when at least one criterion is failed as above: a report that fails no criterion cannot be unlikely to be included.
- "decisive_criteria": the codes of the criteria that decide.

Reports may be written in any language: apply the criteria to their content.
